      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-i/q1-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"[B1] List the five pairs totalling four [M1] use the first binomial model [M1] use the second binomial model [A1] multiply the probabilities in each pair [A1] add the products (c) If Amber makes n calls, require 1 −0.85n &gt; 0.99. [B1] [A1] Rearranging gives 0.85n &lt; 0.01, hence n log(0.85) &lt; log(0.01). Division by the negative logarithm reverses the inequality: Then n &gt; log(0.01)/ log(0.85) ≈28.34, [A1] so the least number of calls is 29 . [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 1 is 17 marks.</p>"
    ],
