    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-i/q6",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-i/q6-question-1.svg\" alt=\"The proportion of tiles with minor faults produced in a factory is thought to be 10% . The factory manager believes that the proportion is higher due to the old machinery. He inspects a random sample of 20 tiles. a) Stating your hypotheses clearly, find the critical region to test the manager’s belief, at the 5% level of significance. ( ) 6 b) State the actual significance level for a test using the critical region of part (a). ( ) 1 Four faulty tiles were found in the sample. c) Complete the test. ( ) 2\" style=\"--madas-width:572.0px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-i/q6-question-2.svg\" alt=\"Question diagram\" style=\"--madas-width:572.0px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-i/q6-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Let p be the population proportion of faulty tiles: Test H0 : p = 0.10 against H1 : p &gt; 0.10. (a) Under H0, X ∼B(20, 0.10). [B1] [A1] For a discrete upper tail subtract the preceding cumulative probability: P(X ≥4) = 1 −P(X ≤3) and P(X ≥5) = 1 −P(X ≤4). The upper tails are P(X ≥4) ≈0.13295 and P(X ≥5) ≈0.04317. [M1] [A1] Thus the critical region is X ≥5 . [M1] [A1] (b) Its actual significance level is 4.317% (approximately) . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-i/q6-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"(c) Four is outside the critical region: Fail to reject H0. [A1] There is insufficient evidence at 5% that the proportion exceeds 10% . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:666.7px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 6 is 9 marks.</p>"
    ],
