    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-i/q7",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-i/q7-question-1.svg\" alt=\"Arnie and Ned play each other in a darts match, which consists of up to three games. The winner is the first person to win two games. The probability that Arnie wins the first game is 0.7 . Whenever Arnie wins a game the probability he wins the next is 0.6 . Whenever Ned wins a game the probability he wins the next is 0.8. a) Find the probability that Arnie wins the match. ( ) 6 b) Given that Arnie won the match find the probability he won in two games. ( ) 2\" style=\"--madas-width:573.3px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-i/q7-question-2.svg\" alt=\"c) Given Arnie won the first game find the probability he won the match. ( ) 3\" style=\"--madas-width:581.3px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-i/q7-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Drawing the tree diagram from Arnie’s point of view: Win 0.6 Win 0.4 0.2 Win 0.7 0.8 Lose Lose 0.6 Win 0.3 Win 0.4 0.2 Lose Lose 0.8 Lose (a) The tree shows the conditional win and loss probabilities from Arnie’s point of view: Stop each path once a player has won two games: [B1] Arnie wins along three paths: Two wins in succession have probability 0.7(0.6) = 0.42. [A1] Win, loss, win has probability 0.7(0.4)(0.2) = 0.056. [A1] Loss, win, win has probability 0.3(0.2)(0.6) = 0.036. [A1] These paths are disjoint, so add them:\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-i/q7-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"P(Arnie wins) = 0.42 + 0.056 + 0.036. [A1] This is 0.512 = 64/125. [A1] (b) Write the conditional probability as P(wins in two and wins match)/ P(wins match). A two-game win is already a match win: Only the first path wins in two games: Restricting to matches won by Arnie gives 0.42/0.512, [B1] or 105/128 ≈0.8203. [A1] (c) The winning paths that start with a win have probability 0.42 + 0.056 = 0.476. [M1] Divide by the probability of winning the first game, 0.7. [A1] The required probability is 0.476/0.7 = 0.68 = 17/25. [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 7 is 11 marks.</p>"
    ],
